social media has become part of our  nature we post we share we like we  follow we unfollow we subscribe but how  is social media affecting us a small  study of twenty college students found a  correlation between students who  demonstrated higher levels of Facebook  addiction and activity in the amygdala  striatal system while they interacted  with Facebook signals this activation  was the same seen in those with  substance addiction in a 2016 UCLA study  teens were split into two groups one was  shown a photo with the high number of  likes and the other group was shown the  same photo but with fewer likes those  who viewed the photo with more likes  were more likely to like the photo  themselves than the group who saw the  photo it's fewer likes again it was the  same picture this experiment found  activity in multiple parts of the brain  when teens saw photos they took receive  a lot of likes in particular the study  found significant activity in part of  the brain's reward circuitry known as  the nucleus accumbens dopamine is  released in the brain after positive  social stimuli such as likes and  positive comments on social media that  photos she just posted she got a bunch  of likes releasing dopamine into her  brain and causing a feeling of  satisfaction  [Music]  psychologist BF Skinner found that mice  would respond to certain stimuli they  knew resulted in our award more often  when the reward came at variable times  this theory can also be applied to  social media and the times we check it  looking for a reward in terms of a like  comment or message we habitually check  our accounts but we are not always  rewarded maybe this photo didn't get the  amount of life's you wanted so you will  try again and again and again looking  for that reward but wait something else  have you ever felt your phone vibrate  what it really didn't according to  robert rosenberger of the Georgia  Institute of Technology we become so  connected to our phones that they've  sort of become part of our bodies  anytime something stimulates our  triggers sensation in an area where you  keep your phone you might believe it's  your phone this is called phantom  vibration syndrome  studies show that nearly 90% of people  report experiencing these phantom  vibrations  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]
 

